net.tailscale
  Join a tailnet with Tailscale, for a private path to SSH
  Applies to this host.

Why
  A server reachable only through a private network has no SSH port for the
  internet to scan or brute force. Tailscale gives this host an address in your
  tailnet, so you reach the same sshd over it, with the same keys;
  firewall.inbound can then close public SSH.

Params (in the module's profile table)
  auth_key_file (default "")
      A file holding an auth key, owned by root with mode 0600, not a symlink,
      in directories only root can write to; `tailscale up` reads it, vps-ready
      never does. Use a one-off or pre-approved key, tagged and not ephemeral: a
      tagged node has key expiry off, and an ephemeral one is removed from the
      tailnet when it goes idle. On a tailnet with device approval, a key that
      is not pre-approved makes the login time out, and a one-off key is spent.
      Delete the file after the login; later applies use the node's own login.
      Empty: log in by hand with `sudo tailscale up --accept-dns=false`.
  login_server (default "")
      The coordination server's URL, e.g. a Headscale server's. Empty:
      Tailscale's.
  hostname (default "")
      This node's name in the tailnet. Empty: the host name.
  auto_update (default true)
      Let unattended-upgrades (updates.auto) install Tailscale's updates; each
      restarts tailscaled, which drops tailnet connections for a moment.

Fixes these audit controls
  none
